eu environment ministers have agreed on  the need to green up the eu's economic  governance system the european semester  and europe's 2020 growth strategy for  the coming decade  at their council in luxembourg the  ministers underlined that environment  policies are important to solving the  challenges faced by eu economies  in particular they noted the potential  for green growth and employment that  could be created by encouraging more  resource efficiency  shifting taxes from labor to pollution  could help create more jobs while green  infrastructure could also be developed  and environmentally harmful subsidies  phased out  the ministers also held a public debate  on waste management eu nations produced  a total of 2.5 billion tons of waste in  2011.  if we want to  use resources efficiently and if we want  to have a circular economy then we've  got to produce  less waste  we've got to cut production of waste  too much waste is wasteful to go in a  phrase  of the roughly half a tonne of waste  produced by each eu citizen annually  only around 40 percent is recycled  most of that recycled waste is used as  landfill the rest is usually incinerated  this wastage deprives the european  economy of many important secondary  materials like metals wood glass paper  and plastic
 

